Q: Select(and copy/edit) text from VS code Integrated terminal output(buffer scrollback) via keyboard A: <p>Source: <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/issues/182210" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/microsoft/vscode/issues/182210</a></p>
<p>There are a few ways:</p>
<ol>
<li><p><code>Shift+tab</code> edit directly in integrated terminal, <strong>if you have</strong> <code>&quot;editor.accessibilitySupport&quot;: &quot;on&quot;</code> in your <code>settings.json</code>.
Vs code command for it is called: <code>workbench.action.terminal.focusAccessibleBuffer</code> and you can invoke it from command palette.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Create your own shortcut to edit terminal output directly in VS Code editor (instead of the terminal itself).
First add this shortcut to your <code>keybindings.json</code>:</p>
</li>
</ol>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>{
    &quot;key&quot;: &quot;ctrl+alt+a&quot;,
    &quot;command&quot;: &quot;runCommands&quot;,
    &quot;args&quot;: {
      &quot;commands&quot;: [
        {
          &quot;command&quot;: &quot;workbench.action.terminal.selectAll&quot;,
        },
        {
          &quot;command&quot;: &quot;editor.action.clipboardCopyAction&quot;
        },
        {
          &quot;command&quot;: &quot;workbench.action.files.newUntitledFile&quot;
        },
        {
          &quot;command&quot;: &quot;editor.action.clipboardPasteAction&quot;
        }
      ]
    }
  }
</code></pre>
<p>After that, when in the terminal, press <strong>ctrl+alt+a</strong> to edit all terminal output in VS Code newly created unsaved file.</p>

