Q: Dynamic Programming Optimal &quot;Non-Decreasing Sequence&quot; A: <p>It is not necessary to simulate any actual operations. After any number of operations, each element in the array is formed by replacing some non-empty subarray with its sum. Thus, this problem is equivalent to finding the maximum number of subarrays we can partition the given array into, such that the subarray sums are non-decreasing.</p>
<p>For each index <code>i</code> in the array, we can use dynamic programming to calculate the maximum number of subarrays we can partition the elements up to index <code>i</code> into, as well as the minimum last subarray sum of such a partition. This can be done by trying all possible split points for a total complexity of <code>O(N^2)</code>. Finally, the minimum number of operations is simply the difference between <code>N</code> and the maximum number of subarrays we could partition the whole array into.</p>
<p>Here is a C++ implementation:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;vector&gt;
int main() {
    std::cin.tie(nullptr);
    std::ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    int n;
    std::cin &gt;&gt; n;
    std::vector&lt;int&gt; nums(1), maxParts(n + 1), minEnd(n + 1);
    for (int x; std::cin &gt;&gt; x;) nums.push_back(x);
    for (int i = 1; i &lt;= n; ++i)
        for (int j = i, sum{}; j; --j) {
            sum += nums[j];
            if (minEnd[j - 1] &lt;= sum &amp;&amp; maxParts[j - 1] + 1 &gt; maxParts[i]) {
                maxParts[i] = maxParts[j - 1] + 1;
                minEnd[i] = sum;
            }
        }
    std::cout &lt;&lt; n - maxParts[n] &lt;&lt; '\n';
}
</code></pre>
